        "problem": "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{4} \\left(\\sqrt x - \\dfrac 2 {x^2}\\right) \\, \\textrm d x .$",
        "worked_solution": "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int_1^4 \\left( \\sqrt x - \\dfrac 2 {x^2} \\right) \\, \\textrm dx = \\int_1^4 \\left( x^{1/2}-2x^{-2} \\right) \\, \\textrm dx.\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we split the integral into the difference of two integrals and factor out the constants. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}(\\sqrt{x}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}{x}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}x-2{\\int }_{1}^{4}{x}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3/2}\\,{|}_{1}^{4}+2{x}^{-1}\\,{|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}({4}^{3/2}-{1}^{3/2})+2({4}^{-1}-{1}^{-1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(8-1)+2(\\frac{1}{4}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{3}+2\\cdot (-\\frac{3}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{3}-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        "screenshot": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004086/example-416.png"
